# Chroma library, modern Earth: THE SPHERES OF SOCIETY, title moments of sphere titles set a. Library thread,
# 2026-10-09, "v22" update stage 4, item 15 (chroma-packs/spheres/title-gaps.md, "The 28 new titles").
# An everyday pair per new title of titles.py (lay judge, paralegal, lawyer, mediator, community centre manager,
# events promoter, DJ) that only its holder meets (holds:), in the style of earth-titles-careers.lib: the first moment
# grows from the title's turning point, the second is a later stretch of the same post.
# Syntax: header of build.py. Rules: SPEC.md. Every moment carries sphere: <sphere>, every option the face it acts in
# (a cross option X>Y the means colour's face). ladder: is the rung the title sits on in its face's ladder
# (character.jobs, read rung by rung); haunt: where the post is done at a haunt among its regulars.
# Evenness: every one-colour option has chance 75 and every cross option 65, so each colour's mean is the same.
# Pairs (complementary calls; cross cycles s1 x4, s2 x4, s3 x3, s4 x3):
#   lay judge  WU (s1) / BRG (s2)     paralegal  UB (s3) / WRG (s4)     lawyer  UR (s1) / WBG (s2)
#   mediator   GW (s3) / UBR (s4)     community centre manager  WR (s1) / UBG (s2)
#   events promoter  BG (s3) / WUR (s4)     DJ  BR (s1) / WUG (s2)
# holds: names the new titles, which are not yet in earth_perks_titles.py (they wait for NEW_TITLES to be merged).
# Nightlife moments (events promoter, DJ) start at 18, young_adult.

# ================================================================ lay judge (rule.W)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- everyday, calls WU (cycle s1); partner: a known name on the morning list

== the fine she could not pay
tier: everyday
sphere: rule
ladder: pillar
at: court
rate: 0.5
share: .0025
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 25-75
calls: WU
holds: lay judge
tone: trouble
life: work, community
horizon: moment
roles: colleague
earth: on the bench as a lay judge, a young mother stands before you for a fine she could not pay, and the list is long
times: Most lay judges, a few times a year; unpaid fines are among the commonest cases before a lay bench (estimate)
gap: 0.5-1
scene: {N} sits in the middle of three lay judges. A young mother stands before the bench for a fine she could not pay, now doubled. {colleague} glances at the clock: twelve cases to go before lunch.
scene W: {N} swore to hear every case to the end, the small ones too. The rule sets the penalty, and it also lets the bench ask why.
scene U: {N} reads the file again: the first fine was for a bus ticket, and every letter went to an old address.
scene B: The chair of the bench retires this year, and the court will remember who kept the list moving.
scene R: Her hands are shaking on the rail. {N} wants to stop the whole thing and just talk to her like a person.
scene G: {N} knows her street, and how a debt like this grows in a family that never had one before.
- W | ask her plainly why the fine went unpaid, and hear the whole answer | v: universalism, benevolence | sphere: rule.W | chance: 75
- U | point out to the bench that the letters went to the wrong address | v: universalism, achievement | sphere: rule.U | chance: 75
- B | agree the usual order quickly, and keep the list and the chair happy | v: conformity, security | sphere: rule.B | chance: 75
- R | say out loud that doubling a bus fine helps no one | v: self-direction, universalism | lever: voice | sphere: rule.R | chance: 75
- G | ask whether her family could help her pay it in small weekly sums | v: benevolence, tradition | sphere: rule.G | chance: 75
- W>U | follow the guidance to the letter and work out what she can truly afford | v: conformity, universalism | sphere: rule.W | chance: 65
- U>B | find the rule that lets the court cancel the extra, and cite it | v: achievement, power | sphere: rule.U | chance: 65
- B>R | win the chair round over the papers before the bench votes | v: power, benevolence | sphere: rule.B | chance: 65
- R>G | catch her after court and give her the number of the free advice desk | v: benevolence, stimulation | sphere: rule.R | chance: 65
- G>W | follow the bench's old custom: adjourn a week so she can bring help | v: tradition, universalism | sphere: rule.G | chance: 65
works: The bench cancels the extra and sets a small weekly sum, and the woman breathes out at the rail.
works: Weeks later the court office tells {N} that the fine has been paid in full.
fails: The bench is split, the doubled fine stands, and she leaves without looking up.
fails: {colleague} tells the chair that {N} held up the list, and the next rota puts {N} on the quiet days.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- everyday, calls BRG (cycle s2); partner: the fine she could not pay

== a known name on the morning list
tier: everyday
sphere: rule
ladder: pillar
at: court
rate: 0.5
share: .002
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 25-75
calls: BRG
holds: lay judge
tone: trouble
life: work, community
horizon: week
roles: colleague
earth: the son of a well-known local man is before your bench for drink-driving, and the chair of the bench plays golf with his father
times: Most lay judges in a small town, once or twice in their years on the bench; in a small place the bench often knows the families before it (estimate)
gap: 1-3
scene: The next case is a drink-driving charge, and the name is one the whole town knows: the son of the man who owns the builders' yard. Over coffee, the chair of the bench mentioned playing golf with his father.
scene W: The oath was to hold the strong to the same rule as the weak. Last month this bench banned a delivery driver for the same thing.
scene U: {N} checks the reading in the file: just over the limit, and the machine was due for its yearly test that week.
scene B: The father sponsors half the clubs in town, and a lay judge who crosses him may find a few doors closed.
scene R: {N} feels the heat rise: the young man is grinning at his friends in the public seats.
scene G: The family have run that yard for three generations, and the grandfather taught half the town to drive, {N} included.
- W | give him exactly the ban the delivery driver got last month | v: universalism, conformity | sphere: rule.W | chance: 75
- U | ask the prosecution for the machine's test record before deciding anything | v: universalism, achievement | sphere: rule.U | chance: 75
- B | let the chair lead, and stay on good terms with the family | v: security, power | sphere: rule.B | chance: 75
- R | tell the chair, there and then, to step aside from this case | v: self-direction, universalism | lever: voice | sphere: rule.R | chance: 75
- G | stand down from the case yourself, since you know the family | act: stand down from the case, since they know the family | v: tradition, conformity | lever: exit | sphere: rule.G | chance: 75
- W>B | ask the court's legal adviser, on the record, whether the chair should sit | v: conformity, power | sphere: rule.W | chance: 65
- U>R | lay the two cases side by side and read the dates aloud in court | v: universalism, stimulation | sphere: rule.U | chance: 65
- B>G | settle it quietly among the three of you in the back room | act: settle it quietly among the three of them in the back room | v: security, tradition | sphere: rule.B | chance: 65
- R>W | report the chair's golf remark to the bench's training officer | v: universalism | lever: voice | sphere: rule.R | chance: 65
- G>U | ask the longest-serving lay judges how the bench handled a case like this before | v: tradition, universalism | sphere: rule.G | chance: 65
works: The young man gets the same ban as the delivery driver, and the town hears about it by evening.
works: Another bench takes the case, and no one can say afterwards that it was settled over golf.
fails: The case is put off twice, and by the summer the young man is driving again.
fails: The father stops nodding to {N} in the street, and a club drops {Ns} name from its dinner list.

# ================================================================ paralegal (rule.U)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- everyday, calls UB (cycle s3); partner: the shoebox at the advice evening

== the appeal form on the paralegal's desk
tier: everyday
sphere: rule
ladder: regular
at: employer
rate: 0.5
share: .005
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-70
calls: UB
holds: paralegal
tone: trouble
life: work
horizon: moment
roles: boss, colleague
earth: a family's appeal against eviction must be in by tomorrow, the lawyer who should sign it is away, and the form is on your desk
times: Most paralegals, about once a year; a missed signature before a filing deadline is a routine trouble of a busy legal office (estimate)
gap: 0.8-1
scene: A family's appeal against eviction must reach the tribunal by four tomorrow. The lawyer who should sign it is away and not answering. The finished form is on {Ns} desk.
scene W: A paralegal may not sign for a lawyer. The rule is there to protect clients like this one.
scene U: {N} knows that tribunal's rules better than most of the firm, and there may be a way to ask for more time that nobody uses.
scene B: {boss} has said that a paralegal who saves a case gets noticed when the training places are handed out.
scene R: The family's eldest daughter has rung twice this morning, and {N} can still hear her voice.
scene G: {N} grew up four streets from that family's flat, and the whole block is watching how this goes.
- W | ring every partner in the firm until one agrees to sign today | v: conformity, benevolence | sphere: rule.W | chance: 75
- U | file the tribunal's little-known request for extra time | v: achievement | sphere: rule.U | chance: 75
- B | take it to the office head, making sure they know who did the work | v: achievement, power | sphere: rule.B | chance: 75
- R | drive the form to the tribunal yourself and ask the desk to hold it | act: drive the form to the tribunal and ask the desk to hold it | v: stimulation, benevolence | sphere: rule.R | chance: 75
- G | ask the family's own advice centre whether one of its lawyers can sign | v: benevolence, tradition | sphere: rule.G | chance: 75
- W>R | stay on the phone to the tribunal until a clerk agrees to hold the case | v: conformity, stimulation | sphere: rule.W | chance: 65
- U>G | write the family a plain letter on what happens if the form is late | v: benevolence, universalism | sphere: rule.U | chance: 65
- B>W | get the senior partner to sign it, and note that it was their choice | v: security, conformity | sphere: rule.B | chance: 65
- R>U | read the tribunal rules through the night for any way to file it unsigned | v: stimulation, achievement | sphere: rule.R | chance: 65
- G>B | ask the old partner who knew the family's late father to sign it as a favour | v: tradition, power | sphere: rule.G | chance: 65
works: The appeal reaches the tribunal with an hour to spare, and the family stay in the flat for now.
works: {boss} hears how the deadline was saved and puts {Ns} name down for the next training place.
fails: The form arrives a day late, and the tribunal will not hear the appeal.
fails: The tribunal writes to the firm about how the form was filed, and {boss} blames {N}.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- everyday, calls WRG (cycle s4); partner: the appeal form on the paralegal's desk

== the shoebox at the advice evening
tier: everyday
sphere: rule
ladder: regular
at: employer
rate: 0.5
share: .004
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-70
calls: WRG
holds: paralegal
tone: mixed
life: work, community
horizon: months
roles: boss, grandparent
earth: every week an old man brings a shoebox of unopened letters to the free advice evening you staff, and the firm allows twenty minutes a client
times: Most paralegals who staff an advice desk, for some months of their years there; the same few clients come back week after week (estimate)
gap: 1-3
scene: On the firm's free advice evening {N} sees a client every twenty minutes. Every week the same old man comes with a shoebox of letters about his pension, most of them never opened.
scene W: The twenty minutes are the rule so that everyone in the queue is seen. Tonight the queue is out of the door.
scene U: Somewhere in that box is the letter that explains the whole thing, and {N} has not had the time to find it.
scene B: The firm runs the evening for its good name, and {boss} counts clients seen, not problems solved.
scene R: The man says sorry every week for being a nuisance, and every week it gets harder to send him home.
scene G: He reminds {N} of {grandparent}, who also kept every letter in a box and opened none of them.
- W | keep to his twenty minutes, and book him in again for next week | v: conformity, universalism | sphere: rule.W | chance: 75
- U | take the box home for a night and sort the letters by date | v: achievement, benevolence | sphere: rule.U | chance: 75
- B | refer him to a paid service, so the evening moves faster | v: achievement, security | sphere: rule.B | chance: 75
- R | stay past closing and go through the whole box with him tonight | v: benevolence, stimulation | sphere: rule.R | chance: 75
- G | ask whether a son or daughter could come along with him next time | v: benevolence, tradition | sphere: rule.G | chance: 75
- W>G | ask the firm to give him a regular slot of his own each month | v: benevolence, conformity | sphere: rule.W | chance: 65
- U>W | write him a one-page list of what to bring and in what order | v: universalism | sphere: rule.U | chance: 65
- B>U | tell the office head the case could bring a paying claim, and ask for hours | v: power, achievement | sphere: rule.B | chance: 65
- R>B | ring the pension office from the desk and refuse to hang up until someone helps | v: stimulation, power | sphere: rule.R | chance: 65
- G>R | walk him to the bus with his box and promise to see it through | v: benevolence, tradition | sphere: rule.G | chance: 65
works: In the box is the letter that explains everything, and his pension is put right by the spring.
works: The old man brings {N} a cake to the next advice evening, and the whole queue claps.
fails: The box goes back and forth for months, and nothing in it is ever settled.
fails: {boss} sees the hours on the sheet and tells {N} the advice evening is not a charity.

# ================================================================ lawyer (rule.U)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- everyday, calls UR (cycle s1); partner: the partnership and the legal aid day

== the copy in the client's own file
tier: everyday
sphere: rule
haunt: rule.counsel
ladder: known
at: court
rate: 0.5
share: .005
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 23-80
calls: UR
holds: lawyer
tone: trouble
life: work
horizon: week
roles: boss, mentor
earth: the firm's best client swears a contract was lost years ago, and in the client's own old file is a copy that would sink their case
times: Most lawyers who argue cases, once or a few times in a career; a client's own papers that hurt their case are a classic test of a lawyer's duty to the court (estimate)
gap: 2-6
scene: The firm's best client swears in writing that the contract was lost years ago. In the client's own old file, in {Ns} desk drawer, is a copy that would sink their case. The hearing is on Monday.
scene W: A lawyer may fight hard for a client but may never let the court be misled. {N} took an oath on that.
scene U: {N} reads the copy twice: one clause in small print decides the whole dispute.
scene B: The client brings the firm more work than anyone, and {boss} has made it plain who keeps the partners paid.
scene R: The other side is a small family firm, and {N} can picture their faces on Monday if the copy stays in the drawer.
scene G: {mentor}, who trained {N}, used to say that a lawyer has only one thing to sell, and that is a good name.
- W | tell the client the copy must go to the other side before Monday | v: universalism, conformity | sphere: rule.W | chance: 75
- U | read the clause closely and build the best honest case that survives it | v: achievement | sphere: rule.U | chance: 75
- B | say nothing unless the court asks, and let the client carry the risk | v: power, security | closed: approval: keeping back a document the court should see; backfire: the copy turns up and the court asks who knew | mark: hid a wrong | sphere: rule.B | chance: 75
- R | ring the client tonight and tell them straight that a lie will lose | v: self-direction, universalism | sphere: rule.R | chance: 75
- G | ask your old trainer over supper what they would have done | act: ask their old trainer over supper what they would have done | v: tradition | sphere: rule.G | chance: 75
- W>U | write the client a careful letter setting out their duty to the court | v: conformity, universalism | sphere: rule.W | chance: 65
- U>B | advise the client to settle now, before the copy can hurt them | v: security, achievement | sphere: rule.U | chance: 65
- B>R | threaten to drop the client unless they agree to come clean | v: power, self-direction | sphere: rule.B | chance: 65
- R>G | give up the case rather than stand up in court for a lie | v: self-direction, tradition | lever: exit | sphere: rule.R | chance: 65
- G>W | hand the copy to the senior partner and let the firm decide as a firm | v: tradition, conformity | sphere: rule.G | chance: 65
works: The client agrees to settle, grumbling, and the firm's name stays clean.
works: The judge never learns how close it came, and {mentor} sends {N} a short note: well done.
fails: The client moves all their work to another firm, and {boss} asks {N} to explain why.
fails: The copy turns up at the hearing anyway, and the judge's questions land on {N}.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- everyday, calls WBG (cycle s2); partner: the copy in the client's own file

== the partnership and the legal aid day
tier: everyday
sphere: rule
haunt: rule.counsel
ladder: known
at: court
rate: 0.5
share: .003
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 23-80
calls: WBG
holds: lawyer
tone: mixed
life: work, community
horizon: years
roles: boss, colleague
earth: the firm offers you a partnership, on condition that you give up the low-paid legal aid cases you have taken for years
times: Some lawyers, once in a career; legal aid work pays far below private rates, and firms weigh it against their profits (estimate)
gap: 3-10
scene: The partners offer {N} a share in the firm at last. There is one condition: {N} must give up the legal aid cases, which pay a third of the going rate and fill a day a week.
scene W: Everyone has the right to a lawyer, and on legal aid day {N} is often the only one who will take the case.
scene U: {N} has run the numbers twice: the legal aid day loses the firm money, but it is where {N} learned most of what {N} knows.
scene B: A partnership means a share of the profits, a name on the door and a say in who is hired.
scene R: The partners talk about the legal aid clients as if they were a bad smell, and it makes {N} want to walk out.
scene G: Half the town's families have come through {Ns} door on legal aid day, and now they send their children too.
- W | keep the legal aid day, and turn the partnership down | v: universalism, benevolence | mark: turned down a chance | sphere: rule.W | chance: 75
- U | show the partners how many paying clients the legal aid day brings in | v: achievement | sphere: rule.U | chance: 75
- B | take the partnership and hand the legal aid list to a junior | v: power, achievement | sphere: rule.B | chance: 75
- R | tell the partners what you think of the condition, and leave for a smaller firm | act: tell the partners what they think of the condition, and leave for a smaller firm | v: self-direction | lever: exit | sphere: rule.R | chance: 75
- G | ask the families who rely on you, and only then decide | act: ask the families who rely on them, and only then decide | v: tradition, benevolence | sphere: rule.G | chance: 75
- W>B | accept, and write the legal aid day into the partnership terms | v: power, universalism | sphere: rule.W | chance: 65
- U>R | set up an evening clinic of your own outside the firm | act: set up an evening clinic of their own outside the firm | v: self-direction, achievement | lever: found | sphere: rule.U | chance: 65
- B>G | take the share, and quietly keep a few old families on as private favours | v: power, benevolence | sphere: rule.B | chance: 65
- R>W | raise the condition at the partners' meeting in front of everyone | v: universalism | lever: voice | sphere: rule.R | chance: 65
- G>U | ask the retired founder of the firm what the legal aid day was for | v: tradition | sphere: rule.G | chance: 65
works: The partners give way, and {Ns} name goes on the door with the legal aid day still in the diary.
works: Years later, a family {N} helped on legal aid sends their eldest to {N} to train.
fails: The partnership goes to {colleague}, and {N} stays where {N} was for another five years.
fails: The legal aid list goes to a junior who cannot keep up, and the families stop coming.

# ================================================================ mediator (rule.G)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- everyday, calls GW (cycle s3); partner: the agreement the firm already wrote

== two old friends split the building firm
tier: everyday
sphere: rule
ladder: pillar
rate: 0.5
share: .0004
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 25-80
calls: GW
holds: mediator
tone: mixed
life: work
horizon: moment
roles: colleague
earth: two partners, friends since school, are splitting up their building firm, and each wants the van, the name and the customers
times: Most mediators of business disputes, a few times a year; small partnerships often end in a quarrel over the shared assets (estimate)
gap: 0.5-1
scene: Two partners sit at {Ns} table to split up the building firm they started together. Each wants the van, the firm's name and the customers. Their wives and brothers are waiting in the corridor.
scene W: {N} promised both men the same hearing and the same rules: no shouting, and each speaks to the end.
scene U: {N} has the firm's books open: the customers are worth more than the van and the name put together, and that changes the sums.
scene B: The larger partner's accountant recommended {N}, and both men know it.
scene R: The two have not looked at each other once in an hour. {N} would like to shake them both.
scene G: They have been friends since they were seven, and their mothers still sit together at church on Sundays.
- W | keep to the rules: each man speaks in turn, and no one interrupts | v: universalism, conformity | sphere: rule.W | chance: 75
- U | lay out three ways the firm could be split, with the sums for each | v: achievement, universalism | sphere: rule.U | chance: 75
- B | remind them what a court case would cost, and let the fear do its work | v: power, security | sphere: rule.B | chance: 75
- R | stop the session and tell them what you see: two boys still fighting | act: stop the session and tell them what they see: two boys still fighting | v: self-direction, stimulation | sphere: rule.R | chance: 75
- G | ask each man to tell the story of the firm's first job together | v: tradition, benevolence | sphere: rule.G | chance: 75
- W>R | let each partner say, uninterrupted, what he is really angry about | v: universalism, stimulation | sphere: rule.W | chance: 65
- U>G | list which customers each partner brought in, and divide them that way | v: tradition, achievement | sphere: rule.U | chance: 65
- B>W | declare in the room who recommended you, so both can trust the process | act: declare in the room who recommended them, so both can trust the process | v: universalism, security | sphere: rule.B | chance: 65
- R>U | meet each partner alone and press him hard on what he truly needs | v: stimulation, achievement | sphere: rule.R | chance: 65
- G>B | bring in the old foreman, the one man both partners still listen to | v: tradition, power | sphere: rule.G | chance: 65
works: The partners sign: one keeps the name and the van, the other the customers in his half of town.
works: At the door the two men shake hands, and their families go to the pub together.
fails: One partner walks out, and the split goes to court after all.
fails: They sign, but one of them tells the whole trade that {N} was on the other side.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- everyday, calls UBR (cycle s4); partner: two old friends split the building firm

== the agreement the firm already wrote
tier: everyday
sphere: rule
ladder: pillar
at: employer
rate: 0.5
share: .0003
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 25-80
calls: UBR
holds: mediator
tone: trouble
life: work
horizon: week
roles: colleague
earth: a firm hires you to settle a complaint between a manager and a worker, and its own agreement is drafted before the first session
times: Some mediators of workplace disputes, once or twice a year; the employer who pays the fee often has a settlement in mind (estimate)
gap: 1-2
scene: A firm pays {N} to settle a complaint between a manager and the warehouse worker who reported him. Before the first session, the firm sends over the agreement it wants signed, with a clause that the worker never speaks of it again.
scene W: A mediator serves both sides, or neither. {N} has said so at every first session for years.
scene U: {N} reads the draft: it settles nothing the worker complained of, and it binds only one side.
scene B: The firm has three more disputes on its books, and it pays well and on time.
scene R: The worker's voice was shaking on the phone. {N} does not like being used as a rubber stamp.
scene G: Most of the warehouse live on the same estate, and whatever happens in that room will be on every doorstep by the weekend.
- W | tell the firm the draft is set aside, and start from both sides' own words | v: universalism | sphere: rule.W | chance: 75
- U | ask both sides in writing what an agreement would have to fix | v: universalism, achievement | sphere: rule.U | chance: 75
- B | take the fee, use the draft as a starting point, and keep the client | v: security, power | sphere: rule.B | chance: 75
- R | hand the case back and tell the firm exactly why | v: self-direction | lever: exit | sphere: rule.R | chance: 75
- G | meet the worker first in the estate's community room, on their own ground | v: benevolence, tradition | sphere: rule.G | chance: 75
- W>G | let the worker bring a friend from the warehouse to every session | v: benevolence, universalism | sphere: rule.W | chance: 65
- U>W | show both sides the draft openly, and strike the one-sided clause together | v: universalism | sphere: rule.U | chance: 65
- B>U | agree to the firm's timetable in return for a free hand on the terms | v: power, achievement | sphere: rule.B | chance: 65
- R>B | tell the firm that a rigged deal will cost it far more at a tribunal | v: power, self-direction | sphere: rule.R | chance: 65
- G>R | sit the manager and the worker down over tea and let them have it out | v: tradition, stimulation | sphere: rule.G | chance: 65
works: The worker gets an apology and a move off the manager's shift, and neither side is bound to silence.
works: The firm grumbles, then books {N} for its next dispute on {Ns} own terms.
fails: The firm finds another mediator who will use its draft, and the worker signs.
fails: The worker sees {N} arrive with the firm's papers and refuses to say a word.

